Falsifies `block_roster::write_then_read_returns_the_same_devices_in_order`.

The defect: `write` stores each device's ordinal in the transport half of its
entry, so the transport is lost and read back as the ordinal. The page still has
the right magic, count and capacity, so every header check and every test that
compares only counts passes; the kernel would publish a roster whose drives all
sit on the wrong bus.

Expected to fail: `assert_eq!(roster.get(i), Some(devices[i]))`, on any list whose
ordinal differs from its transport.


diff --git a/crates/block_roster/src/lib.rs b/crates/block_roster/src/lib.rs
--- a/crates/block_roster/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/block_roster/src/lib.rs
@@ -190,7 +190,7 @@
     for (i, d) in devices.iter().enumerate() {
         let at = HEADER_BYTES + i * ENTRY_BYTES;
         page[at..at + 4].copy_from_slice(&d.ordinal.to_le_bytes());
-        page[at + 4..at + 8].copy_from_slice(&d.transport.to_le_bytes());
+        page[at + 4..at + 8].copy_from_slice(&d.ordinal.to_le_bytes());
     }
     Ok(())
 }
